## Setting Up the H2 Database

1. No manual setup required
   The application uses the embedded H2 database engine. All database files are stored in:

2. Configure your H2 JDBC connection
In `dbFunction.java`, ensure your JDBC URL looks like this:
```java
  String url = "jdbc:h2:./data/bankingdbz";
        String name = "bnk";
        String password = "";

3. Run the application
Compile and run the BankingApp class which is in the app folder. The app will connect to the H2 database automatically.

4. View the database contents
You can use the H2 Console (GUI) or any tool that supports .db files if you want to inspect your data.


## Using MySQL Database (For Production)

1. Install and configure MySQL server.
2. Create a database schema and user for the banking system.
3. Update JDBC connection settings in your configuration or dbFunction.java accordingly.
4. Run the application. It will connect to the MySQL database for persistent storage.


## Running the Application
    Compile and run the Main class from the app package.
    Follow on-screen prompts in the console UI to register, login, and manage accounts.

## Testing
    Unit tests are located in the test package and use JUnit.
    Integration tests cover database operations.
    Run tests using your IDE or build tool (e.g., Maven, Gradle).

